from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "2. The Word “Apostle”"),
("p", "Claim: the word “apostle” means one who is sent, and the New Testament uses it of more than one kind of person. A reader who takes the word to name a single office in every place will find passages he cannot place; a reader who takes it to mean only a messenger will find passages that give it a weight no messenger carries. The first task is to see how the word is used."),
("sub", "2.1 The word and its meaning"),
("p", "The Greek word translated “apostle” is //apostolos//, from a verb meaning to send. The King James Version renders it “apostle” most of the time and “messenger” in two places. It is a word with a plain sense, one sent out, and the plain sense is the Lord’s own: “The servant is not greater than his lord; neither he that is sent greater than he that sent him” (John 13:16). The word that is rendered “he that is sent” in that verse is the same word."),
("tbl", "John 13:16 and the plain sense of the word.", [
    ("John 13:16", T("John 13:16"), "The one sent is not greater than the one who sends him."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "2.2 The word used of the Lord"),
("p", "The Letter to the Hebrews uses the word of Christ himself: “Wherefore, holy brethren, partakers of the heavenly calling, consider the Apostle and High Priest of our profession, Christ Jesus” (Hebrews 3:1). The letter is written to Hebrew believers, and the verse names the Lord as the one sent from the Father. This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting. The verse shows how wide the word runs: it names the Son who was sent as well as the men he sent."),
("sub", "2.3 The word used of messengers"),
("p", "Twice the King James Version renders the word “messenger.” Paul writes of Epaphroditus, “my brother, and companion in labour, and fellowsoldier, but your messenger, and he that ministered to my wants” (Philippians 2:25). He writes of the brethren who carried the collection from the churches, “they are the messengers of the churches, and the glory of Christ” (2 Corinthians 8:23). In both places the Greek is //apostolos//. The translators judged that the persons were sent by the churches and not by the Lord, and they chose the English word to say so."),
("tbl", "The two places where the translators wrote “messenger.”", [
    ("Philippians 2:25", T("Phil 2:25"), "Epaphroditus is the Philippians’ messenger to Paul."),
    ("2 Corinthians 8:23", T("2 Cor 8:23"), "The brethren are the messengers of the churches."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "2.4 What the usage shows"),
("p", "The word, then, runs from the plain sense of one sent by a church, through men sent out as companions of Paul, to the Twelve whom the Lord named and to Paul whom the risen Lord made. The translators saw the range and wrote “messenger” where the sender was a church. The volume follows the same line. It asks, of each use, who sent the man, and it does not carry the weight of the Twelve into a passage where the sender is a church, or the plain sense of the word into a passage where the sender is the Lord."),
("p", "The question of whether the others called apostles held an office is therefore not settled by the word. It is settled, where it can be settled, by what the passage says about the sender and the commission. Chapter 3 takes the Twelve, chapter 4 the others, and chapter 5 Paul."),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The word means one sent, and the Lord used it so in John 13:16.",
    "It is used of Christ himself in Hebrews 3:1.",
    "The translators wrote “messenger” where the sender was a church.",
    "The weight of the word depends on who sends and what is committed.",
  ]),
]
